\par\par \b \uc2 HK dollar seen buoyant as shares rise, yuan eyed.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ovais Subhani   \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2003\par 09:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Dec 22 (Reuters) - With Hong Kong's stock market strong and many investors punting on a rise in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts expect the Hong Kong dollar to hold above its pegged minimum against the dollar for at least some months.   \par \par \uc2 A key issue is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's apparent decision to leave money more freely available in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  banking system after its periodic \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, making it easier for people to borrow and buy shares.   \par \par \uc2 "Hong Kong has long been a no-growth, deflationary economy," said David Simmonds, strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland in Singapore, referring to slow growth and falling consumer prices in the past 60 months.   \par \par \uc2 "All of a sudden you have got nirvana: a weak U.S. dollar, low interest rates, a hot Chinese economy, and increased willingness among investors to park cash in Hong Kong.   \par \par \uc2 "This strikes me as being as decent a combination as you can get. So why give all that up? They should keep the policy a bit flexible for now at least," he said.   \par \par \uc2 To keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too far and hurting exports, Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has, since September, bought billions of U.S. dollars. It exchanges them for Hong Kong dollars, which then sit in bank accounts, available for lending. It sold HK$16 billion ($2.1 billion) between December 12 and 19 alone.   \par \par \uc2 UNSTERILE   \par \par \uc2 Central banks normally "sterilise" that liquidity effect by selling short-term securities, taking cash back out of the system to match the inflow.   \par \par \uc2 But Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has evidently not sterilised all of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lately. Falling interest rates show that money is more freely available, propping up the share market, whose rallies have attracted people to buy more Hong Kong dollars.   \par \par \uc2 Although that has tended to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a little higher, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts such as Simmonds think the scheme suits \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy, which could do with low interest rates and a bit of market optimism. So they reckon the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain buoyant for a while yet.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar since late September has strengthened above the minimum rate of 7.80 per U.S. dollar that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has defended since 1997. It was around 7.7650 per dollar on Monday, down from an October high of 7.6920.   \par \par \uc2 On Monday, Hong Kong's official interbank rate was 0.14955 percent, against 1.14625 in the United States.   \par \par \uc2 IPO FLOWS    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A factor pushing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  higher has been speculation that China\par would raise the value of the yuan            , in the face of trade\par pressure from the United States.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One year yuan non-deliverable forwards , which reflect expectations of the future direction of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traded on Monday at a rate suggesting a yuan at 7.83 per U.S. dollar in a year's time, from the current peg at 8.28 per dollar.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong dollar forwards were trading at an implied rate of 7.2 per U.S. dollar in a year's time.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another has been rising optimism about the Hong Kong economy, which has combined with the easy availability of money in drawing investors into the share market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark Hang Seng Index has climbed 50 percent from its low for the year in April, to hit highs last seen in July 2001.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A flood of investments into large initial public offerings of shares - such as the $3.46 billion offering of China Life shares, the largest IPO this year - has further boosted Hong Kong dollar demand.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the territory could have pushed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back to the peg if it had wanted to, because it could have fully sterilised its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Ideally, the way it should work is that because U.S. rates are higher, funds should be flowing out of Hong Kong and taking advantage of higher U.S. rates. But that is not happening," said Claudio Piron, strategist at Standard Chartered Bank. (US$1=HK$7.76).    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031222dzcm001eu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
