\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Defends Policy Of Allowing Stronger HK Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2003\par 12:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Though Hong Kong has kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strictly pegged to the U.S. dollar for 20 years, even the biggest champion of that regime still appreciates the virtues of flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 A previously little-known quirk of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system - the fact it only prevents a weakening, not a strengthening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has allowed the market exchange rate of the Hong Kong dollar to become stronger than its peg of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar since late September. The pair was trading at about 7.7650 late Thursday, and has gone as low as 7.70.  \par \par \uc2 The newfound volatility in the Hong Kong dollar rate has prompted calls from some observers for the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to institute a stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, one that would work on both the strong and weak sides of the peg.  \par \par \uc2 But the HKMA shows no sign of changing its mind, as it seems to appreciate the advantages of having that flexibility on the strong side. Rather than changing the system, it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally in the market, selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars and buying U.S. dollars, to keep the rate from drifting too far from the peg.  \par \par \uc2 In a report issued Thursday, the HKMA pointed out that the strengthening of the exchange was due in part to improved sentiment toward Hong Kong, and had had no ill effects on the local economy.  \par \par \uc2 "The sudden shift in market sentiment reflects improved fundamentals in Hong Kong, as well as the recent weakness of the US dollar and market speculation about an appreciation of the renminbi (or yuan)," the HKMA said in a report issued Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "This had minimal macroeconomic implications," it said in its new Monetary and Financial Stability Report.  \par \par \uc2 What's more, the HKMA suggested, having room for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate has prevented local interest rates from falling even lower than they already have. Though the low interest rates help stimulate lending, they can hurt banks' profitability if they are sustained.  \par \par \uc2 "The adjustment of the exchange rate has probably also been affected by the limited room for falls in interest rates, which were already at a low level," it said. "Should the spot rate be kept close to 7.8, interest rates would have to decline much more."  \par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the HKMA concluded, the flexibility now built into the Hong Kong dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board hasn't harmed investors' confidence in the system.  \par \par \uc2 "It does not appear that the absence of a convertibility undertaking on the strong side of the link has had any effect on the credibility and effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system," it said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031218dzci000ie\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
