\par\par \b \uc2 HK Dlr Mkts Late: Forwards Discount Widens On Liquidity  \b0\par\par\uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2003\par 09:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The discount on Hong Kong dollar forwards widened Thursday, as brief worries over a new SARS case in Taiwan gave way to a flood of interbank liquidity brought on by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The excess funds in the interbank market pushed down local interest rates, widening the gap between Hong Kong and U.S. interest rates, thus leading to a deeper discount on forwards. The benchmark one-year forward contract was quoted at 530 to 510 below the spot rate, compared with 500 to 480 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Health officials in Taiwan had said Wednesday that a medical researcher had contracted SARS, or severe acute respiratory syndrome, apparently in the process of studying the disease. The news spooked investors in Taiwan and other Asian markets, and caused Hong Kong forward discounts to narrow Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 But by Thursday they had widened again, as funds from the HKMA's recent sales of Hong Kong dollars entered the market. The aggregate balance, a measure of interbank liquidity, surged to HK$24.17 billion Wednesday, from HK$15.3 billion, and is forecast to expand further to HK$27.81 billion Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "The focus of the market therefore shifts back to excess liquidity," one trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA sold a comparatively small HK$559 million into the market overnight Wednesday, which followed a massive HK$7.94 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the HKMA's sales, which would tend to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are aimed at relieving the upward pressure from the recent big purchases of Hong Kong dollars by foreign investors interested in Hong Kong stocks.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was, however, little moved at HK$7.7645, compared with HK$7.7638 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong interbank offered rates declined with the continued influx of liquidity from the HKMA. The one-year Hibor was quoted at 0.75%, from 0.79% Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Hong Kong Exchange Fund notes rose slightly, in line with similar changes in U.S. Treasurys. The two-year note was quoted at 1.21%, up from 1.19% previously. The 10-year note was unchanged at 4.38%.  \par \par \uc2 The market is still digesting the unexpected fall in U.S. consumer prices in November, which showed there is little pressure on the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates, as inflation hasn't yet taken hold.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                   Latest        Late Wednesday \par Overnight Hibor   0.65%         1.00% \par 1-mo Hibor        0.23%         0.28% \par 3-mo Hibor        0.21%         0.28% \par 1-yr Hibor        0.75%         0.79% \par USD/HKD Spot      HK$7.7645     HK$7.7638 \par 6-mo HKD Fwd      -360 to -340  -345 to -335 \par 1-yr HKD Fwd      -530 to -510  -500 to -480 \par  \par 4.32% EF Note Due Dec. 2, 2013: \par  \par    Price: 99.93 Vs 99.93 \par    Yield: 4.38% Vs 4.38% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1053 4178  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031218dzci000aw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
