\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec 17  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2003\par 10:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Markets in Taiwan and Singapore were rattled early Wednesday as the specter of SARS returned to haunt Asia, but investors recovered their composure after officials downplayed the likelihood of another outbreak of the deadly disease. ("=WRAP: New SARS Case Jolts Mkts; But Outbreak Unlikely," ran at 0649 GMT; final WRAP with updates will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Despite the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing the size and frequency of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has shown few signs of giving up its recent strength as the market easily absorbs a flood of Hong Kong dollars from the HKMA.(=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA will run around 0930 GMT; see also "HKMA Sells HK Dlrs Overnight Tues; Day's Total HK$7.94B" that ran at 0125 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China Wednesday urged Taiwan to remove discriminatory restrictions and unreasonable obstacles to allow postal, telecommunications and trade links to be restored to the mainland as soon as possible. ("China/Links -2: China Issues New Taiwan Policy Document" ran at 0344 GMT; see also "China Vows To Smash Any Taiwan Independence Bid" that ran at 0650 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's fixed-asset investment growth slowed slightly in the first eleven months of 2003, but the continuing high rate of investment continues to pose a threat to China's long-term economic stability, economists said Wednesday. ("=DATA VIEW: China Fixed Asset Invest Rise Fans Econ Fears" ran at 0638 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's ruling coalition is scheduled to approve tax and pension reform proposals that will call for keeping in place tax breaks on home loans and increasing the amount that employees and employers have to pay for national pension. (story will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's exports rose at a sharply lower than expected pace in November due to a surprise drop in electronics exports, but the island's economic recovery remains on track, data released Wednesday show. Non-oil domestic exports, a key barometer of Singapore's economic health, increased 2.3% to S$9.72 billion last month from a year ago. The growth was significantly weaker than the 20.6% rise in October and the 11.3% expansion that economists had predicted in a Dow Jones Newswires poll. ("DATA VIEW: Singapore Nov Exports Rise Slower Vs Oct" ran at 0716 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK O4/JGB: The fate of Japan's cyclical economic recovery will determine how high Japanese government bond yields rise in 2004 although a continued ultra-easy monetary policy will limit the increase. ("=OUTLOOK 04: JGB Market To Focus On Econ Recovery In 2004" ran at 0520 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: The consensus forecasts for 2004 paint a picture of an Australian economy posting solid growth, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remaining firm and only a light tap on the interest rate brakes. But there are risks. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Several Risks To 2004 Forecasts" ran at 0447 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's recent growth in exports was fueled by increased overseas demand, particularly from China, rather than a weaker won, a senior Bank of Korea official said Wednesday. ("Bk Of Korea: Overseas Demand, Not Won, Helping Exports" ran at 0204 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's residential real-estate boom shows little sign of easing with house prices continuing to appreciate in November, according to data from the Real Estate Institute Inc. released Wednesday. ("NZ's Median House Price -2: Auckland Shows On Month Fall" ran at 2259 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar held its ground against the yen in Asia Thursday on bids suspected to have been placed by Japanese monetary authorities in the low-Y107s, despite selling by local banks on behalf of commercial customers. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 :Dlr/Yen Holds Firm On Suspected Japan MOF Bids" ran at 0534 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Honda Motor said Wednesday it aims to sell 1.4 million vehicles in the key U.S. market in 2004, up 3.7% from its estimated sales of 1.35 million units there this year, and is also more optimistic about its sales prospects in the domestic market. The Japanese car maker also said it will target global vehicle sales of 3.2 million units in 2004, up 10% from its overall sales estimate for this year. ("Honda -2: More Optimistic On Domestic Sales Growth In '04" ran at 0232 GMT; WRAP will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian funds manager and life insurer AMP Ltd. said Wednesday it has completed a A$650 million institutional bookbuild priced at A$4.30 a share. The bookbuild is being used to make up for a huge shortfall in the company's A$1.2 billion rights offer, which in turn is being used to redeem AMP's reset preferred securities. ("=WRAP: Australia's AMP Completes A$650 Million Bookbuild" ran at 0759 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: LG Card's eight main creditor banks will be invited to bid for a controlling stake in the troubled credit card issuer Dec. 31, main creditor Woori Bank said. The bidders will be limited to the eight creditor banks that have pledged KRW2 trillion of loans in recent LG Card bailouts, and creditors will be asked to each offer a bid of at least 1 trillion won for the stake. ("Korea Creditor Bks To Be Asked To Bid For LG Card Dec 31" ran at 0009 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Life looks set for a strong start when its shares debut on the Hong Kong stock exchange Thursday, given its huge growth potential. China's largest life insurer offered 6.47 billion shares at HK$3.625 each to raise US$3.01 billion, in the world's biggest initial public offering this year. (story will run around 1000 GMT; see also "=China Life Faces Selling Pressure From Retail Investors" that ran at 0556 GMT with "CORRECTION: China Life IPO Raises US$3.01B, Not US$3.04B" at 0832 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Quanta Display said Wednesday it now expects to post a pretax profit of NT$150 million in 2003, a dramatic improvement from an earlier prediction of a NT$2.5 billion loss. The forecast revision follows similar moves by other Taiwanese flat-panel display makers, which have been enjoying strong demand for their products amid a recovery in the personal computer sector. (WRAP will run by around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: One by one, Hong Kong's four mobile operators have delayed, scaled back or toned down their once-heady enthusiasm toward third-generation wireless, ostensibly because of a shortage of handsets. But the bottleneck has actually done Hong Kong's operators a favor: the 3G business plan has yet to be proven, and in a city with such tight competition, could be a costly exercise in a still untested technology. (story will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian Airline System Bhd. will keep focusing on premium travel, even as rivals Singapore Airlines and Thai Airways prepare to launch budget carriers, company officials said in an interview. ("=INTERVIEW: Malaysian Air Stays Plush, Shuns Budget Model" ran at 0845 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A major industrial cleaning and chemicals corporation China National Bluestar (Group) Corp., a state-run company with little experience in automobile production, has been tagged as the preferred party to take over South Korea's fourth-largest auto maker, Ssangyong Motor. (story on company and strategy behind its bid may run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Japanese government is in talks with electronics makers, including NEC and Hitachi, to jointly develop next-generation routers for use by telecom carriers, a government official said Wednesday. ("Japan Govt In Talks With NEC, Hitachi To Develop Routers" ran at 0323 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Brilliance China Automotive said Wednesday it has agreed to buy out a government entity's stake in one of its subsidiaries, a move that will increase the company's shareholding in its joint venture with German luxury carmaker BMW. After the purchase, which will cost Brilliance China 135 million yuan, the company's effective interest in the joint venture will increase to 49% from 44.6%. ("Brilliance China To Buy Out Govt Share In Its BMW Venture" ran at 0032 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai television broadcaster iTV PCL said Wednesday it plans to raise 3 billion baht by selling new shares to a major soap opera producer and a popular television host in a bid to attract a larger audience and reduce retained losses.("Thai iTV To Offer New Shrs To Local Partners At THB10/Shr" ran at 0401 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/ASIAN STOCKS-TECHNICAL ANALYSIS: Asian stock markets will have a firm bias in 2004, but don't expect the kind of dramatic gains that were seen during the bull run of 2003. That's the message from technical analysis of major Asian stock indices, which shows that while there will be little downside in 2004, much of the year will probably be spent in consolidation. ("=OUTLOOK 04: Charts Imply Firm, Not Red-Hot Asia Stocks" ran at 0758 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, said it will crack down on financial firms found to be illegally trading in overseas shares, namely Hong Kong-listed stocks, major financial dailies reported Wednesday. ("China \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Regulator Targets Cos Illegally Trading HK Stks" ran at 0453 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean auto leasing company Hyundai Capital said Wednesday it has issued $228 million in five-year asset-backed securities at a yield of 4.98%. The manager for the issue is Standard Chartered Bank. ("S Korea's Hyundai Cap/ABS -2: 11th Issue This Year" ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Sovereign Asset Management Wednesday reiterated it won't sell its shares in South Korea's SK Corp. to other parties to seek management control of SK Corp. ("Sovereign: Won't Sell Stake In SK Corp To Other Parties" ran at 0213 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Textile company Texwinca Holdings said its first-half net profit fell 41% on year to HK$180.1 million as higher cotton yarn costs to weighed on earnings. ("HK Texwinca/Earnings -2: Higher Cotton Yarn Prices" ran at 0626 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shares of cross-media business information services provider HC International opened 13% higher at HK$1.23 in their debut Wednesday, from their IPO price of HK$1.09. (HK HC International/Debut -2: Eighth Most Actively Traded" ran at 0212 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Carter Holt Harvey, a New Zealand timber processor and exporter, said Wednesday that Moody's Investors Service has changed the outlook on its long-term senior unsecured credit rating to positive from stable, reflecting a solid financial performance over 2003 and a NZ$658 million reduction in debt since the end of 2001. ("NZ Carter Holt/Moody's -2: Reflects Solid Performance" ran at 2344 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: The Indonesian government said it may sell a 10% stake in tin miner PT Timah next year amid strong global tin prices, possibly through a secondary public offer. The government owns a 65% stake in the world's largest integrated tin miner. ("Indonesia Govt May Sell 10% Stake In Timah Next Year" ran at 0130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Aluminum Corp. of China, better known as Chalco, said the company is considering placing new H shares, though no definitive plan has been agreed upon. ("China Chalco/Placement Plan -2: Share Price Down 8.2% Tue" ran at 0003 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: There was a time when calls to five-star hotels in India would bring bargain offers for rooms with free meals and spa treatments. But the hotel industry is now enjoying a boom in demand - the result of the country's rapid economic growth, which has boosted business and leisure travel. ("=India Hotels Boom On Higher Tourist, Executive Arrivals" ran at 0754 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Hafei Aviation Industry said Wednesday it completed the maiden flight of its first regional jet jointly produced with Harbin Aircraft Industry (Group) Co. and Brazil's Empresa Brasileira de Aeronautica SA, or Embraer. ("China Hafei, Brazil Embraer JV Flies First Regional Jet" ran at 0704 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/JAPAN POWER TRADING: Decision time for power trading in Japan is rapidly approaching, but it's still far from clear when, and if compromises on rules, regulations and structures can be hammered in time to meet government deadlines. ("Outlook 04: Tough Decisions Ahead On Japan Power Trading" ran at 0204 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Australia's Woodside Petroleum has trumped U.S. oil major Exxon Mobil in a US$77 million deal to clinch majority ownership of Mauritania's emerging offshore oil province, people with knowledge of the deal told Dow Jones Newswires. ("=WRAP: Australia's Woodside Beats Exxon To Africa Acreage" ran at 0831 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: China is abandoning import quotas for all refined oil products from next month, in line with World Trade Organization obligations, but to protect the interests of Chinese refiners, the government will be using other mechanisms to control imports. ("=CHINA ENERGY WATCH:Oil Product Import Rules Sidestep WTO" ran at 0753 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/THAI SUGAR INDUSTRY: Thailand's sugar industry is bracing for a tough year ahead in 2004, as shrinking demand from Russia could worsen a domestic glut at a time when production is expected to hit another record as forecast by industry experts. ("=OUTLOOK 04:Thai Sugar Faces Bleak Future Amid Oversupply" ran at 0344 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031217dzch000d2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
