\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Sells HK Dlrs Overnight Tues; Day's Total HK$7.94B  \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2003\par 02:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to sell Hong Kong dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during London and New York trading hours Tuesday, in its heaviest day of market activity yet as it worked to keep the Hong Kong dollar from strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA sold HK$4.04 billion during Hong Kong trading hours Tuesday. But according to figures provided to financial data systems, it went on to sell HK$3.9 billion more after hours, bringing the total to HK$7.94 billion in the day - buying more than US$1 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The spot exchange rate was little moved early Wednesday, with the U.S. dollar quoted at HK$7.7628. That implies that the HKMA's actions are simply neutralizing massive purchases of Hong Kong dollars by others, for instance by subscribers to a recent US$1.9 billion share placement by BOC Hong Kong (Holdings) Ltd. (2388.HK).  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system automatically prevents the local unit from weakening away from the peg rate, but there is no such provision in the system to prevent it from strengthening - only the HKMA's discretion to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in individual transactions.  \par \par \uc2 Since late September, the Hong Kong dollar has been trading at a rate stronger than its official peg to the U.S. dollar of HK$7.80. That unusual situation has been driven by a combination of strong flows of foreign investment into Hong Kong assets, and speculation on the possible appreciation of the Chinese yuan, using the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar as a proxy.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally in the markets since then to prevent further strengthening of the Hong Kong dollar, usually to keep the U.S. dollar from falling below HK$7.76. It has so far sold a total of HK$26.48 billion, buying about US$3.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031217dzch0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
