\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Sells HK$3.07B More Overnight; Monday Total HK$4.57B  \b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2003\par 02:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold an additional HK$3.07 billion into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during London and New York trading hours Monday, capping its largest single day of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar first strengthened above its peg in September.  \par \par \uc2 The sales of Hong Kong dollars, and purchases of U.S. dollars, are intended to prevent the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening further against the U.S. dollar. Added to the HK$1.5 billion the HKMA sold during Hong Kong trading hours Monday, the day's total comes to HK$4.57 billion, buying about US$590 million.  \par \par \uc2 Yet the additional sales Monday, like an earlier round of large sales Friday, had little obvious effect on the market exchange rate. The U.S. dollar was quoted around HK$7.7630 early Tuesday, the same level it was trading at for most of Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Since late September, the Hong Kong dollar has been trading at a rate stronger than its official peg to the U.S. dollar of HK$7.80, an unusual situation driven by a combination of flows of foreign investment into Hong Kong assets, and speculation on the possible appreciation of the yuan, using the Hong Kong dollar as a proxy.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system automatically prevents the local unit from weakening away from the peg rate, but there is no such provision in the system to prevent it from strengthening - only the HKMA's discretion to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in individual transactions.  \par \par \uc2 The latest \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  bring the HKMA's \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales since the September strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a grand total of HK$18.54 billion, which would buy just under US$2.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA provides information on its market actions to financial data systems, but doesn't comment on them other than to say it acts as needed to counter market volatility.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Corrected December 15, 2003 23:12 ET (04:12 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 The day's total comes to HK$4.57 billion, buying about US$590 million.  \par \par \uc2 (In an item timed at 0106 GMT, the size of the U.S. dollar purchase was misstated.)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031216dzcg00011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
