\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: HKMA Sells HK$4.04 Bln In  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Tuesday  \b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2003\par 10:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday sold a total of HK$4.04 billion, buying around US$520 million, in several transactions aimed at easing any possible gain in the Hong Kong dollar against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The sales continue what has been an unusually active few days for the HKMA, which acts as the city's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and implementer of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. It sold HK$4.57 billion Monday and HK$2.96 billion last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "The HKMA might have detected some significant fund inflows and has therefore been selling the Hong Kong dollar in sizable amounts to keep it from rising too much above the peg rate of HK$7.80," a trader said.  \par \par \uc2 In response to Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar weakened very slightly. Late in the Hong Kong trading day, the U.S. dollar was quoted at HK$7.7635, compared with HK$7.7627 earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants suspect the recent HKMA sales were largely absorbed by foreign investors buying Hong Kong dollars, for instance to participate in local initial public offerings.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system automatically prevents the local unit from weakening away from the peg rate, but there is no such provision in the system to prevent it from strengthening - only the HKMA's discretion to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in individual transactions.  \par \par \uc2 Since late September, the Hong Kong dollar has been trading at a rate stronger than its official peg to the U.S. dollar of HK$7.80. That unusual situation has been driven by a combination of strong flows of foreign investment into Hong Kong assets, and speculation on the possible appreciation of the Chinese yuan, using the Hong Kong dollar as a proxy.  \par \par \uc2 So as it has this week, the HKMA has been acting occasionally to do what it calls preventing market volatility. It has sold a total of HK$22.58 billion since September, including Tuesday's sales, buying about US$2.9 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Yet most market participants don't anticipate a return to the peg rate until the fund inflows to Hong Kong, which have been supporting the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reverse.  \par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the HKMA's sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been adding huge amounts of liquidity to an already flush interbank market, pushing down local interest rates. The one-year Hong Kong interbank offered rate, or Hibor, was quoted at 0.80% late Tuesday, down from 0.98% in the middle of last week.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson and Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031216dzcg000fa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
