\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec 16  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2003\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan will compile an extra budget for the current fiscal year to finance necessary spending increases, though the modest budget won't require any new government bond issues, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Tuesday. ("Japan Tanigaki-2: Cabinet To OK MOF Budget Drafts Dec 20" first take ran at 0059 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Bank of Japan's policy board Tuesday voted unanimously to hold monetary policy steady for the third consecutive time, as expected, judging that the country's recovering economy doesn't need extra stimulus. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also slightly upgraded its economic assessment for the fourth consecutive month, citing an improvement in personal spending. ("BOJ/Policy Unchanged -2: In Line With Expectations" ran at 0238 GMT; "BOJ/Economy -2: Upgrade Only Slight, Focus On Consumption" ran at 0609 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold an additional HK$2.14 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Tuesday afternoon, buying about US$275 million, in its latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar against its U.S. counterpart. ("HKMA/\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -2: Brings Day's Total To HK$3.26 Bln" ran at 0828 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan will make sure it doesn't run out of funds for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and will keep an eye out for any sharp gains in the yen, a top Japanese Finance Ministry official said Tuesday, reiterating the ministry's resolve to counter yen strength. ("Japan MOF Mizoguchi -2: Closely Monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market" ran at 0104 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean banks' combined net profit is expected to decline this year due to credit card woes but sector earnings will improve in 2004, said the Bank of Korea. ("South Korean Banks' Net Profit To Fall To KRW1T In 2003" ran at 0343 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Sales of locally made cars in China soared 70% on year between January and November to 1.73 million units, the China Association of Automobile Manufacturers said Tuesday. In the first 11 months, more than 4 million vehicles were produced domestically, up 35% on year, and 1.80 million cars were made, up 83.7% on year, the association said. ("China-Made Cars Jan-Nov Sales Up 70% On Yr To 1.73M Units" ran at 0311 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's total foreign debt at the end of September rose 9.22% compared with end-December 2002 to $184.09 billion, the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported Tuesday. ("China End-Sep Foreign Debt -2: Short-Term Debt Up $14.42B" ran at 0235 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: As developed and developing nations around the world wring their hands over the loss of ever more jobs to China, Hong Kong is wondering whether it can somehow buck that trend and use its growing economic integration with the mainland to create jobs, not destroy them. ("=Hong Kong Wonders If China's Growth Will Bring More Jobs" ran at 0845 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's unemployment rate likely eased further to below 8% in the September-November period as the tourism sector and domestic consumption continued to improve. Data due around 0815 GMT Thursday. ("=PREVIEW: HK Unemployment Likely Fell Below 8% In Sep-Nov" ran at 0329 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi Tuesday said the government will issue by year end long-awaited details of the new tax structure for local and imported cars. ("Malaysia/Auto Tariff -2: Import Duties To Be Reduced" ran at 0257 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The State Bank of Vietnam takes a big step Wednesday in modernizing its monetary system, issuing a bank note worth five times as much as any now circulating - about $32. The 500,000 dong bill, complete with anti-counterfeiting measures not built into existing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will offer Vietnamese people more than just a chance to carry a slimmer wallet - it will give them a reason to use the money in it. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: New Dong Note A Vote Of Confidence For Vietnam" ran at 0816 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The Asian Development Bank has approved its largest loan to China of $500 million, to partly finance a $2.36 billion railway linking the industrial eastern cities to the poorer western interior regions of China. ("ADB Extends $500M Loan For China's East-West Railway" ran at 0320 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's economy is expected to have accelerated in the third quarter, with demand for consumer goods and new houses kept alight by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holding interest rates at a stimulatory level. GDP data due Friday. ("=PREVIEW: NZ 3Q GDP To Rebound Spurred By Low Rates" ran at 0222 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar fell to a new record low against the euro Tuesday in Asia, as U.S. and European fund buying triggered stop loss orders. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Falls To New Record Low Against Euro" ran at 0544 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Boral Ltd. launched a A$840 million hostile takeover bid for rival Adelaide Brighton on Tuesday, which will cement its stronghold in the Australian building materials market. ("=WRAP: Australia's Boral Unveils A$840M Bid For Rival" ran at 0825 GMT; "=INTERVIEW: Australia's Boral May Issue US Bonds" ran at 0535 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Fresh from its largest ever acquisition just last week, Australian blood products company CSL Ltd. agreed to sell its animal health vaccines business to Pfizer Inc. on Tuesday for about A$170 million in cash. The divestment of CSL's smallest unit, flagged by the company two months ago and due to be completed by March 2004, comes on the heels of its purchase of Aventis SA's plasma unit for up to US$925 million. ("=WRAP: Australia's CSL Sells Animal Health Unit To Pfizer" ran at 0613 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's LG Group Tuesday said it could sell its stakes in LG Card and LG Investment & Securities to creditors - a move that would strip the country's second largest conglomerate of its financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether. ("S Korea LG Grp May Sell Fincl Units' Stakes To Creditors" ran at 0111 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Samsung SDI said Tuesday it will invest 583 billion won to build a new plasma display panel production line at its plant in Chonan, south of Seoul, which will enable the company to almost double its PDP production capacity. ("S Korea Samsung SDI/Invest -2: From Jan-Oct 2004" ran at 0139 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shareholders of China Telecom approved its plan to buy six provincial phone networks from its parent, China's largest fixed-line telecom operator. The transfer of assets will take the number of provinces in which it operates to 10 from four and lift its subscriber base by 45.1 million to 107.3 million. China Telecom is paying its parent CNY46 billion and assuming CNY34 billion of debt. ("China Telecom Shareholders Approve Purchase Of Networks" ran at 2353 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Isuzu Motors said Tuesday it's considering assembling trucks in Ukraine in an effort to develop new overseas markets. This could lead to the establishment of the first production base set up by a Japanese truck manufacturer in that region of the former Soviet Union. ("Isuzu Mulls Truck Assembly In Ukraine To Tap Growth Mkts" ran at 0321 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: A ferocious rainstorm in Melbourne earlier this month forced Australian insurer Promina Group to back away Tuesday from its most bullish recent earnings guidance. The cost of these claims, net of reinsurance recoveries, will be about A$37 million for the current year ending Dec. 31, it said. The Dec. 2 storm is also likely to affect market forecasts for other general insurers such as Insurance Australia Group and Suncorp-Metway. ("=WRAP: Melbourne Storm Rains On Australia's Promina Group" ran at 0536 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's Cochlear Tuesday said interim and full-year earnings are on the skids due to increased U.S. competition, less than two months after the world's largest maker of hearing implants reassured investors it expects solid growth. ("=WRAP: Australia's Cochlear Dives After Profit Warning" ran at 0558 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Fresh from a major Trans-Tasman acquisition, Australian & New Zealand Banking Group still wants to play a bigger role in Asia, with hopes of securing a key stake in a suburban Shanghai credit union that may soon become China's newest bank. ("=INTERVIEW: Australia's ANZ Eyes Shanghai Banking Role" ran at 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BRISBANE: Australian discount airline Virgin Blue Holdings Tuesday vowed to protect its market share, which is nudging 30%, from rival Qantas Airways and its offshoot Jetstar. ("=INTERVIEW: Virgin Blue Market Share On The Increase" ran at 0504 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: A change in the way Hyflux accounts for revenue from a desalination plant will cut the Singapore water treatment company's full-year revenue for 2003 by as much as S$10 million (US$1=S$1.7082), Chief Executive Olivia Lum said. ("=INTERVIEW:Singapore Hyflux/Rev -2: Desalination Project" ran at 0419 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: BOC Hong Kong (Holdings) Ltd. confirmed Tuesday its parent is selling about US$1.9 billion of old shares, or 10.12% of the blue-chip bank's issued share capital. Bank of China is placing 1.07 billion shares to independent shareholders at HK$13.7 apiece, representing a 12.2% discount to its HK$15.6 last trading price before suspension Monday. Upon completion of the share sale, Bank of China's stake in its overseas-listed unit will be cut to 65.82% from 75.95%. (spot on wires)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Great Wall Automobile said Tuesday the sponsor of its initial public offering, BNP Paribas Peregrine, has exercised in full its overallotment option requiring the auto maker to issue 17.1 million additional new shares at the IPO price of HK$13.30, equivalent to 3.76% of its existing share capital and 3.62% of the share capital after being enlarged by the issue of new shares. Net proceeds of HK$222 million from the issue will be used as working capital. ("China Great Wall Auto IPO Overallotment Option Exercised" ran at 0024 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's biggest airline Cathay Pacific Airways said Tuesday it is looking into setting up a low-cost carrier, in response to plans by its competitors in Asia to launch budget airlines. "Setting up a separate carrier is one of a wide range of options we're looking at...(but) it's only one option," said Cathay's corporate development director Tony Tyler. ("HK Cathay Pacific Looking Into Low-Cost Carrier Option" ran at 0245 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indofood Sukses Makmur confirmed Tuesday that its chief executive, Eva Riyanti Hutapea, has tendered her resignation. The company gave no reason for her resignation but local newspaper Koran Tempo reported that Hutapea's resignation is due to a conflict with the Salim family, who controls the company through First Pacific Co. Indofood, the world's largest instant noodle producer, says it expects to name a new CEO in May. ("Indonesia/Indofood -2: No Reasons Given For Resignation" ran at 0903 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong publishing firm Oriental Press Group said it is considering converting its existing headquarters into a three-star hotel when its new head office is completed next year. It may also lease or sell the HK$358.4 million Kowloon Bay-based building to boost its income. ("HK Oriental Press May Convert Old Head Office Into Hotel" ran at 0205 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: EON Capital remains one of the best buys in Malaysia's banking sector, supported by strong earnings prospects and good asset quality. ("=Asian Stock Focus: EON Cap Attractive On Good Prospects" ran at 0004 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean banks' average capital adequacy ratio stood at 11.3% as of end September, marginally up from 11.03% three months earlier, said the Financial Supervisory Service Tuesday. ("S Korea Banks' Capital Adequacy Ratio Avg 11.3% End-Sep" ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean carrier Korean Air says it plans to cut its investment in 2004 to 843.6 billion won from about KRW1 trillion this year. ("Korean Air/Invest -2: Scales Back 2004 Investment" ran at 0130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The Australian government said Tuesday it will revamp the fuel excise regime, bringing alternative products like liquefied petroleum gas and ethanol into the revenue-raising fold. ("=WRAP: Australian Government Recasts Fuel Excise System" ran at 0700 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Australia's Woodside Petroleum may have discovered a major oil province offshore Mauritania, with analysts estimating that the Tiof field could hold more than 300 million barrels. ("=WRAP: Australia's Woodside May Have New Oil Province" ran at 0714 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: State utility Electricity of Vietnam Corp., or EVN, plans to borrow up to $1.75 billion from local and foreign commercial banks next year to finance the building of a new hydropower plant, a senior EVN official said Tuesday. ("Elec Of Vietnam Seeks $1.75B Loans '04 For New Pwr Plant" ran at 0612 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031216dzcg000dh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
