\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Sells HK$156 Mln More In Additional  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2003\par 10:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold an additional HK$156 million Friday afternoon, buying about US$20 million, in its second transaction of the day to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The sale brings the day's total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to HK$932 million, according to data provided by the HKMA to financial information systems.  \par \par \uc2 Yet Friday's second sale, like the first, didn't have a dramatic effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level. After the transaction, the U.S. dollar was quoted at HK$7.7639, up from HK$7.7630 earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar's spot exchange rate has been stronger than its peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar since late September.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system automatically prevents the local unit from weakening away from the peg rate, but there is no such system to prevent it from strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA has been using occasional sales of Hong Kong dollars to ease the local unit's recent strength, although it hasn't actively tried to force a return to the peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031212dzcc000ct\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
