\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Bonds-Hong Kong markets challenge ties with U.S.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan   \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2003\par 03:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 21 (Reuters) - An unusual combination of factors has driven short term interest rates in Hong Kong well below their U.S. peers, challenging the convention that the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg should mean interest rates are the same.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts and traders say, however, arbitrageurs are unlikely to close the gap in the short term because of heavy investment flows into Hong Kong, the gateway to China. The peg should survive the test in the long run, they add.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pegged to the U.S. dollar for 20 years and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangement that supports the peg requires that interest rates move in parallel.   \par \par \uc2 But the Hong Kong dollar has spent the last two months on the stronger side of its pegged level at 7.8 per U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Overnight rates in Hong Kong are at historic lows and quoting under two basis points, and the spread between one-month swap rates in the United States and Hong Kong has widened to 105 basis point from eight in late-September.   \par \par \uc2 There is no divergence in the longer tenors, suggesting markets do not expect the HK dollar's strength to last.   \par \par \uc2 Ten-year swaps are around 4.6 percent both in Hong Kong and the United States, propped up by expectations of an economic rebound.   \par \par \uc2 "You have a situation in Hong Kong where you have zero interest rates at the short end and, at the same time, the long end is trying to price in U.S. policy tightening towards the end of the next year," said Simon Klassen, fixed income analyst with JP Morgan.   \par \par \uc2 "There is an extra 100 basis points between the Hong Kong and the United States,"   \par \par \uc2 In an ideal situation, such an interest differential would give rise to arbitrage trades, in which investors would exchange their HK dollars for U.S. dollars to earn the higher U.S. yields.   \par \par \uc2 But, foreign money is pouring into Hong Kong for investment in property and in equities, especially Chinese stocks, and for speculation on revaluations in the Chinese yuan.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try and weaken the HK dollar back towards its 7.8 peg. It has injected nearly HK$11 billion since September.   \par \par \uc2 "All those inflows are just getting focused at the short end and have pushed down rates to very low levels," Klassen said.   \par \par \uc2 "There is a natural arbitrage that says that you should be borrowing in HK dollars and investing in the U.S., but that's not the way the flows are and no one's prepared to arbitrage."   \par \par \uc2 STILL STABLE   \par \par \uc2 Mike Moran, economist at Standard Chartered Bank, said this did not mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangement was at any risk. Policy rates, with Hong Kong's base rate at 2.5 percent, were still unchanged, he said.   \par \par \uc2 HKMA chief Joseph Yam said last week he did not mind the decline in rates given Hong Kong was still mired in deflation.   \par \par \uc2 Other analysts agreed that short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves could lead to a temporary disconnect between Hong Kong and U.S. yields.   \par \par \uc2 "At the very short end there can be a temporary decoupling of rates," said Bert Gochet, interest rates strategist with UBS.   \par \par \uc2 "If in the next one year the HK dollar can appreciate or move around in a one percent band, then it means interest rates can be different from the U.S. rates by one percent," he said.   \par \par \uc2 But long term yields in the HK$120 billion exchange fund notes and bills market are rising, reflecting the newfound optimism over U.S. growth and yields.   \par \par \uc2 "In the long run, people will expect the peg to hold at 7.8 and so the long end of the curve cannot differentiate. So you get a steep curve," Gochet said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031121dzbl000f7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
