\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-HK's fin sec says no barrier to more HK$ sales.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Azhar Sukri   \par \uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2003\par 08:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Nov 20 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's Financial Secretary Henry Tang said on Thursday the government would not hesitate to sink more money into the market to stabilise the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the greenback.   \par \par \uc2 "We will continue to monitor the situation. If necessary we will continue to inject further liquidity into the market," Tang told reporters on the sidelines of a luncheon.   \par \par \uc2 Tang made the comments when asked if the government would consider setting a ceiling on the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been pegged at HK$7.80 per U.S. dollar for twenty years.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market since September to weaken the local dollar following an unusual appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar after calls by industrialised nations for more flexible exchange rates.   \par \par \uc2 Inflows of funds for upcoming initial public offerings and speculation over a possible future revaluation of the Chinese yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been cited by analysts as reasons for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.   \par \par \uc2 The appreciation comes despite the fact that the Hong Kong government is facing a record budget deficit this year and with the local economy only showing a modest recovery.   \par \par \uc2 From its pegged exchange rate of HK$7.80 per U.S. dollar, the Hong Kong dollar firmed to about HK$7.70 in late September, triggering \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then gradually headed back towards its peg rate until last week when it strengthened to HK$7.73 per dollar, drawing the HKMA into its latest round of liquidity injections.   \par \par \uc2 The local unit weakened slightly following Tang's comments to HK$7.7591/01 per dollar from its morning level of 7.7585/95.   \par \par \uc2 The HKMA's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from September 25 to date amount to nearly HK$11 billion (US$1.4 billion). Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is becoming less effective due to persistent bearishness on the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The HKMA, the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market seven times so far this week and bought a total of US$337 million. It is obligated to defend the Hong Kong dollar from weakening below its HK$7.80 peg with the U.S. dollar but has no mandate to stop it from strengthening above that level.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031120dzbk00237\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
