\par\par \b \uc2 HK dollar defies fundamentals, stays strong.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2003\par 09:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 19 (Reuters) - Braving heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and an unhealthy economic backdrop, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has surprised markets by straying from a pegged exchange rate and remaining firm for two months.  \par \par \uc2 And now, surging fund flows into domestic money markets have driven short-term interest rates to historic lows and are helping keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strong, both in the spot and forward markets.  \par \par \uc2 Throw in expectations of an appreciation in the tightly managed Chinese renminbi and traders think there is a case for a higher Hong Kong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The HK dollar was quoted around 7.75 per dollar on Wednesday. The two-decade old peg spiked from a fixed level of 7.8 per U.S. dollar in late September after a Group of Seven communique on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies caused the U.S. dollar to slide.  \par \par \uc2 Forwards moved into deeper discounts on the U.S. dollar, with the one-year Hong Kong dollar priced at 7.35 on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The key driver is that there is still a lot of liquidity in Hong Kong that is keeping onshore rates very, very low and that clearly has been driving forwards lower," said Mike Moran, global markets economist with Standard Chartered Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "As a secondary factor, what is happening in China and what is happening to the broader economy is also driving people to take a position on the Hong Kong dollar as well as the renminbi."  \par \par \uc2 "The discount in the forwards isn't necessarily a reflection that people expect the HK dollar to be very strong in the future," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Fundamentally, there is little to support any bullishness. Hong Kong is barely recovering from its third recession in six years, unemployment remains high and deflationary pressures have dogged the economy for five years.  \par \par \uc2 LIQUIDITY SWELLS  \par \par \uc2 Liquidity has been rising as foreign money betting on stocks and on a yuan revaluation poured into the territory.  \par \par \uc2 The stock market is up nearly eight percent in two months. According to Nomura International's data, foreigners have invested over $400 million so far this year in Hong Kong stocks.  \par \par \uc2 Those flows pale in comparison with the funds the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been injecting through its U.S. dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 An asymmetry in the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangement has the HKMA strictly defending its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a decline beyond 7.8 but it is not obliged to stop the HK dollar from rising.  \par \par \uc2 The HK dollar has not moved back to the 7.8 peg level, but inter-bank liquidity has doubled in a week to over HK$10 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Short-term rates have moved Japan-style to near zero, and that keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards at their lows.  \par \par \uc2 CONVOLUTED  \par \par \uc2 Trading in the HK dollar has been full of twists and turns.  \par \par \uc2 For over a year markets were betting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would weaken to converge with the yuan, as the latter appreciated from its nearly pegged levels around 8.3 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At the peak of the frenzied speculation, when the yuan was seen rising seven percent in a year, the bets on the HK dollar were reversed to factor in a higher \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Now, despite the United States imposing quotas on Chinese garments, markets expect the yuan will appreciate just about four percent in a year if its trading band is widened.  \par \par \uc2 They have not scaled back their bullishness on the HK dollar markets. Few are buying U.S. dollars, even though they expect the HK dollar will eventually decline to 7.8 and they earn an interest rate differential on such trades.  \par \par \uc2 "The risk is that there is a larger deviation to the downside and the HKMA has not been particularly effective in stemming that," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan.  \par \par \uc2 "No one is sure where the floor is in U.S.dollar/HK dollar because there are still some positions outstanding and some flows into the market. None of the dealers want to take the risk of buying U.S. dollars, which is why the forwards are this way."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031119dzbj001wn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
