\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Nov 19  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,785 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2003\par 10:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's gross domestic product likely expanded around 4.5% on year in the third quarter, as domestic demand continued to drive one of the fastest-growing economies in Southeast Asia this year, said economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires. (GDP data will run at 1000 GMT; WRAP will run around 1130-1200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: A looming "bra wars" trade spat between the U.S. and China spilled over Wednesday into the market for the yuan, pushing the dollar to a deeper discount against the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the forward market. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: US Protectionism Steepens Dlr Discount To Yuan" ran at 0550 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China regrets the decision by the U.S. to impose trade sanctions on some Chinese-made textile goods, and reserves the right to appeal to the World Trade Organization, a Ministry of Commerce spokesman said Wednesday. China has canceled its second buying trip to the U.S. after the Bush administration imposed trade sanctions on imports of some Chinese textiles. ("China Commerce/Textiles -2:Breach Of WTO Free Trade Rules" ran at 0407 GMT; "China Cancels Buying Trip As U.S. Imposes Trade Sanctions" ran at 0340 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Deputy U.S. Trade Representative Josette Sheeran Shiner defended new U.S. import sanctions on a range of Chinese textile products as "speed bumps" to moderate huge rises in such imports. (story will run around 1020 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's nascent economic rebound could falter if the government carries out burdensome tax reforms proposed for next fiscal year, analysts say, as the effective tax hikes threaten to exacerbate already-sluggish consumer spending trends. ("=Proposed Tax Steps May Threaten Japan's Econ Recovery" ran at 0703 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The People's Bank of China Wednesday issued detailed rules to guide the settlement of yuan transactions following a landmark decision to allow Hong Kong banks to offer personal services using the mainland's strictly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("China's Central Bk Issues Rules For HK Yuan Banking Svcs" ran at 0647 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: A Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject funds into Japan's money market was only partly completed Wednesday, signaling the country's financial system is awash with money, with no indication of tightening liquidity. ("=WRAP: BOJ Undersubscribed Repo Op Shows Money Mkt Awash" ran at 0658 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 COLOMBO: Sri Lanka's 2004 budget will be presented to Parliament from 0830 GMT. (WRAP will run around 1230 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: In what may be the first case of human securitization, Japan Digital Contents says it will securitize a pin-up girl, and will sell Y5 million of securities to individual investors. The investors will also enjoy a free copy of her photo book and get invited to her talk shows as incentive. (story will run around 0930-1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: International appetite for yield was evident Wednesday as two Asian dollar-denominated bond offerings drew such strong responses that one increased the deal, and both tightened/raised their price expectations. (WRAP including Hutchison Whampoa, Kogas and Hyundai Motor offerings will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: France's Renault is offering Y30 billion of five-year Samurai bonds, following Volkswagen Financial Services' successful Y50 billion Samurai sale last week, indicating Japanese invetor appetite toward the yen-denominiated bonds remains strong. ("France's Renault Samurai" ran at 0134 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: In a post-election formality, Junichiro Koizumi easily won approval from the lower house of Parliament Wednesday to stay on as Japan's prime minister. But Koizumi faces a reinvigorated opposition vowing to grill him on his increasingly unpopular plan to send troops to Iraq and efforts to rekindle the world's second-largest economy. Koizumi is expected to retain all of his 17-member Cabinet, which resigned en masse earlier in the day to pave the way for the election.("Japan Parliament/ Koizumi -2: Post-Election Formality" ran at 0543 GMT; more stories through the day)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: Amid the hullabaloo created by the Reserve Bank of Australia resuming its rate tightening cycle, the warning over the future by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Ian Macfarlane has passed virtually unnoticed. Macfarlane stepped outside his normal arena of monetary policy in a speech last week, noting the chance of intergenerational conflict as young savers struggle to make ends meet. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH:RBA Future Warning Goes Largely Unheeded" ran at 0252 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =INSIDE INDOCHINA: Step by cautious step, Vietnam is building a web of bilateral agreements it hopes will help net it membership in the World Trade Organization. The agreements should help to assure Hanoi's trading partners that it is committed to open markets and free trade, and so pave the way for Vietnam's entry into the WTO. But bilateralism may yet prove to be a double-edged sword. ("INSIDE INDOCHINA:Hanoi Sees Bilateral Ties As Way To WTO" ran at 0455 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippine government posted Wednesday a smaller budget deficit in October due to higher revenue collection, snapping target overruns since August. Earlier in the day, trade data showed imports fell for the second month in a row in September on weak electronics imports, indicating that domestic consumption remains sluggish. (WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand house prices continued to accelerate in October, defying predictions of a moderation, and providing more ammunition for those expecting the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates in December. ("NZ's Oct Median House Sale Price" ran at 2259 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ WATCH: Bank of Japan policy makers will likely keep monetary settings steady at their two-day meeting Thursday and Friday despite a sharp fall in stock prices this week. ("=BOJ Watch: Policy Shift Unlikely Despite Stock Mkt Slump" ran at 0508 GMT)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again late Tuesday, in a bid to prevent the U.S. dollar from weakening against the local unit, but the effectiveness of the quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves appears to be waning. The HKMA also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Wednesday afternoon, but traders said the spot rate was almost completely unaffected by the transaction. ("HKMA Moves To Curb HK Dollar Rise Becoming Less Effective" ran at 0444 GMT; "HKMA Sells/Weaken Unit -2: Spot Rate Unaffected By Sale" ran at 0822 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: A leading Indian trade body wants the government to give foreign airlines the right to buy up to a 49% stake in Indian carriers in a new aviation bill expected to be discussed in parliament next month. ("=INTERVIEW: Foreign Airlines Must Be Given Stake In India" ran at 0733 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar edged higher against the yen in Asia Wednesday as widespread rumors Japanese authorities were lurking below the psychologically key Y108 put a floor under the ailing U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0550 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Airways International Wednesday priced its shares at 50 baht apiece ahead of its share offering Thursday and Friday to raise THB22.15 billion, which will be partly used to upgrade the airline's services. The Thai national carrier will offer 285 million new shares and 157.75 million shares held by the Finance Ministry, including a greenshoe option for 57.75 million shares - reducing the government's holding to around 70% from 93%. ("Thai Airways/Price" ran at 0348 GMT and 0504 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Patrick Corp. Wednesday said annual net profit rose 49%, largely due to an earnings boost from discount airline Virgin Blue, and confirmed it could bid for the local arm of U.S.-based RailAmerica Inc. ("=WRAP: Australia's Patrick Soars On Virgin Blue Success" ran at 0625 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's local institutions and deep-pocketed investors have bought a majority of government-sponsored Vayupak Fund I, which was initially aimed at retail players seeking an alternative investment to bank deposits. (WRAP with results and analysts' comments will run after 0930 GMT market close)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's CSR Ltd. said Wednesday a strong Australian dollar and a weak sugar price will combine to damp full-year earnings. The Australian building products, aluminum and sugar company reported first-half net profit of A$100 million from with A$319.4 million a year ago, before the spin off of building materials concern Rinker Group. ("=WRAP: Strong Australian Dlr, Low Sugar Price To Hit CSR" ran at 0524 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Cordless phone and electronic learning product maker VTech Holdings said Wednesday its net profit in the six months ended Sept. 30 fell 65% to US$17.4 million from US$50.1 million a year earlier. ("HK VTech/Earnings-2: Elec Learning Pdt Sales Fall In U.S." ran at 0913 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Qantas Airways chief executive Geoff Dixon said Wednesday he is "very confident" the airline will secure a good price for planes to be used by its domestic budget offshoot. ("Australia's Qantas/Discount Fleet -2: CEO Speaking" ran at 2313 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Hynix Semiconductor said Wednesday it filed a suit against the European Union Nov. 14, claiming that European governments have unfairly imposed import duties on the South Korean chipmaker. ("S Korea Hynix Files Suit Against EU On Tariff Ruling" ran at 0210 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Major creditor banks of LG Card Wednesday said they will provide emergency loans to the credit card firm, if parent LG Group and LG Card are able to come up with a stringent restructuring plan and provide collateral. ("S Korea Banks To Give Loans To LG Card If Co Restructures" ran at 0139 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Economic recovery in Asia has allowed DBS Group Holdings to cut provisions and earn a solid third-quarter profit, but concerns about its core lending business are a key reason investors are advised not to buy the stock of Southeast Asia's largest lender. ("=Asian Stock Focus: DBS Weak Lending Ops Keep Buyers Away" ran at 0001 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Siam City Bank has priced its shares at THB20.60 apiece and warrants at THB1.05 each, in an offering starting Thursday. ("Siam City Bk Sets Share Price At THB20.60;Wt At THB1.05" ran at 0316 GMT  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shares of Sau San Tong Holdings doubled to 50 HK cents in their debut on Hong Kong's Growth Enterprise Market, from with their IPO of 25 HK cents. ("HK Sau San Tong/Debut -2: Ninth Most Actively Traded" ran at 0210 GMT; WRAP will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The South Korean government plans to hold an international auction in December to sell its 9.1% stake in Kookmin Bank, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy Wednesday. ("S Korean Govt To Sell Kookmin Bank Stake In December" ran at 0246 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Troubled orchid grower Euro-Asia Agricultural's provisional liquidators have received a number of formal restructuring proposals from potential investors ("HK Euro-Asia's Liquidators Get Restructuring Proposals" ran at 0322 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mitsui O.S.K. says it has signed a long-term shipping contract for two bulk carriers with Shanghai Baosteel Group Corp., becoming the first non-Chinese company to cut such a deal with China's largest steel group. ("Mitsui O.S.K. Signs Shipping Deal With Shanghai Baosteel" ran at 0308 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Daiwa House Industry said its group net profit for the fiscal first half nearly tripled, citing cost cuts and improvement from the year-earlier write-off of unfunded retirement and pension liabilities. ("Daiwa House -2: Cost Cut,Rebound From Yr-Ago Pension Loss" ran at 0124 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Broad Intelligence said its IPO's retail tranche was about 170 times subscribed and the placement tranche was six times covered. ("HK Broad Intelligence: Retail Tranche 170 Times Covered" ran at 0336 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Mobile phone carrier China Unicom said at end-October it had 14.65 million subscribers to its code division multiple access, or CDMA, network service, compared with 13.34 million CDMA users at end-September. ("China Unicom: CDMA Users At End Oct Reach 14.65 Million" ran at 2347 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Beijing-based supermarket operator Wumart Stores priced its IPO at HK$6.22 per share and exercised the issue's overallotment option on strong demand from retail investors. ("Beijing's Wumart Stores Prices IPO Shares At HK$6.22 Each" ran at 2336 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Bank Rakyat Indonesia, which lends mainly to farmers and small businesses, says its nine-month net profit rose 26% on year to IDR1.82 trillion on higher net interest income. ("Bank Rakyat Indonesia/Earnings -3: Lending Rises 19%" ran at 0135 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Tom.com shareholders Wednesday approved the purchase of a technology firm from one of the media company's substantial shareholders for a total consideration of no more than US$150 million. ("HK Tom.com/Tech Co Buy" ran at 0431 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: General Motors Corp. of the U.S. is betting on strong growth in both China's luxury car market segment and an underdeveloped minicar market for the success of domestically-produced Cadillac and Spark models, a company executive said recently. ("=INTERVIEW:GM Balances Cadillac, Minicar For China Sales" ran at 0842 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Xinjiang Hops, a major barley supplier and beer company in northwestern China, said Wednesday that it has received a formal notice from the China Securities Regulatory Commission, stating it is under investigation. ("China Xinjiang Hops Receives Formal Notice On Probe" ran at 0042 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Torch Investment Co. said Wednesday it has signed an agreement with the China unit of Eaton Corp. of U.S. and Shaanxi Fast Gear Co. to set up a joint venture producing heavy-duty truck transmissions in western Xian city. ("China Torch, Eaton Unit To Set Up Truck Transmission JV" ran at 0148 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Shareholders of Zhejiang Haitong Food Group have approved the plan to set up a confectionery and beverage joint venture with Japan's Itochu in Shanghai. ("China's Haitong Food Shareholders Approve Itochu JV Plan" ran at 0315 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Air New Zealand Chief Executive Ralph Norris said Wednesday the airline's domestic passengers in the first two weeks of November were up 10% from a year earlier. The significance of the announcement is that Air New Zealand launched its discount domestic airline Express in October last year and though the cheaper fares were expected to boost travel, it was uncertain if the increase would be sustained beyond the first year. ("Air NZ/Domestic Travelers -2: 'Outstanding Success'" ran at 0114 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Asian gold investors cautiously waded into waters not tested in more than seven years Wednesday, taking out a key sticking point that could provide bullion with smooth sailing to $420 if not higher, market watchers said. The yellow metal briefly sneaked across the $400 a troy ounce mark Wednesday morning during Asian trade for the first time since March 1996, as investors actively sought out alternatives to the flagging U.S. dollar. ("=Gold To Keep Sparkling Over $400 After Passing Key Test" ran at 0711 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Japanese are expected to use more platinum in 2003 to make their cars environmentally-friendly, while at the same time wearing less platinum jewelry amid a switch to more affordable white gold, according to figures released Tuesday by a leading metals refiner. ("=Japanese Putting More Platinum In Cars, Less On Bodies" ran at 0144 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesian energy minister Purnomo says OPEC should keep output unchanged when the group meets Dec. 4 if the oil price remains firm. ("Indonesia/OPEC Output -2: Price Hovering Above Band" ran at 0246 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Last week, companies from Russia, China and South Korea agreed to push ahead with the building of a natural gas pipeline from eastern Siberia once political endorsement is received, but geopolitical concerns and economics pose major obstacles to the ambitious project. ("=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Knotty Problems In Gas Pipeline Plan" ran at 0813 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Kansai Electric Power says its earnings for the fiscal first half improved, largely thanks to cost-cutting measures, but Japan's second-largest power utility also revised down its profit outlook for the year ending March. ("Kansai Electric Power -2: Cuts FY Profit Estimates" ran at 0640 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031119dzbj000hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
