\par\par \b \uc2 HK Dlr Mkts Late: Hibors Fall Again On Growing Liquidity   \b0\par\par\uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2003\par 09:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong interbank offered rates declined again Tuesday on fresh inflows of liquidity that resulted from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week.   \par \par \uc2 The one-year Hibor was quoted at 0.93%, down from 0.98% Monday, while the three-month Hibor declined to 0.19% from 0.23%.   \par \par \uc2 "Low Hibors will likely continue until the end of this year," a trader said.   \par \par \uc2 The main indicator of surplus interbank liquidity, the aggregate balance, opened at HK$8.19 billion Tuesday, up from HK$5.9 billion late last week - already an unusually high level.   \par \par \uc2 The HKMA's sales last week of Hong Kong dollars, aimed at keeping the U.S. dollar from weakening below the HK$7.76 level, totaled about HK$4.66 billion.   \par \par \uc2 In another sign of excess liquidity, Tuesday's regular auction of short-dated Exchange Fund bills also drew an average accepted yield of -0.01% for the one-month bill.   \par \par \uc2 A negative yield, meaning that the price paid for the bond is higher than its return, usually makes financial sense for an investor only if rates are expected to fall further.   \par \par \uc2 Traders also noted that market makers are required to participate in the HKMA's auction, helping push the yield into the negative. "They bid for the shortest tenor to minimize the risk, while still fulfilling the tender participation requirement," a trader said.   \par \par \uc2 Yields on longer-dated Exchange Fund notes rose somewhat slightly, in line with U.S. Treasurys, on profit-taking, traders said. The two-year note was quoted at 1.36%, up from 1.31% Monday, while the 10-year note rose to 4.26%.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar was little moved in the spot and forward markets. The U.S. dollar was quoted at HK$7.7610, compared with HK$7.7593 Monday. Traders said the HKMA's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week had established clear support at the HK$7.76 level.   \par \par \uc2 The discount on Hong Kong dollar forwards remained wide, reflecting the wide interest rate gap between Hong Kong and the U.S. The one-year forward contract was quoted at 390 to 375 below the spot rate, compared with 390 to 380 Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Widely anticipated news that Hong Kong banks would be allowed to offer services in the Chinese yuan, or renminbi, had little impact on the bond or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. "It's not a big liberalization," another trader said.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa said Tuesday that all locally licensed banks will be permitted to offer basic financial services denominated in yuan to retail customers beginning next year.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                   Latest        Late Monday \par Overnight Hibor   0.005%        0.005% \par 1-mo Hibor        0.06%         0.06% \par 3-mo Hibor        0.19%         0.23% \par 1-yr Hibor        0.93%         0.98% \par USD/HKD Spot      HK$7.7610     HK$7.7593 \par 6-mo HKD Fwd      -300 to -290  -310 to -300 \par 1-yr HKD Fwd      -390 to -375  -390 to -380 \par  \par 3.73% EF Note Due June 5, 2013: \par  \par    Price: 96.23 Vs 96.33 \par    Yield: 4.26% Vs 4.24% \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Batson and Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by John Viljoen   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1053 4178   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031118dzbi000ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\