\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Late Thursday Action Brings Week's Sales To HK$3.9B  \b0\par\par\uc2 206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2003\par 04:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Thursday again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar, capping an unusually active week of market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again when the U.S. dollar dipped below 7.76 against the Hong Kong dollar, implying we may see strong support at that level," a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Together with a previous sale earlier in the day, the HKMA Thursday sold a total of HK$1.55 billion, buying around US$200 million. Its sales for the week now total around HK$3.9 billion, equivalent to buying about US$500 million.  \par \par \uc2 The spot exchange rate of the Hong Kong dollar has been stronger than its official peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar since late September. Friday morning, the U.S. dollar was quoted around HK$7.7630.  \par \par \uc2 That strength has been driven by large inflows of speculative money into Hong Kong, betting on the local stock markets and a possible appreciation in the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031114dzbe0006r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
