\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA says  \highlight22\uc2 intervened \highlight\uc2  to buy US$100mln vs HK$ again.   \b0\par\par\uc2 207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2003\par 14:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar for the second time on Thursday to try to stabilise the strengthening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 In the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which took place around 1050 GMT, the HKMA office in London confirmed the authority had bought around US$100 million.   \par \par \uc2 This was in addition to an earlier move on Thursday when it bought about US$100 million, approximately HK$777 million.   \par \par \uc2 Figures released on Reuters screens showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sixth since late on Monday, and indicated that the HKMA had sold a total of HK$3.879 billion (US$500 million) so far this week.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar was trading at 7.7630/40 to the dollar at 1251 GMT, versus 7.7655/60 at the close of Hong Kong trade on Thursday. It had strengthened to 7.7380/90 in London on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar at HK$7.80 per dollar, but strengthened in September in a rare move triggered by calls from developed nations for more flexible exchange rates in Asia.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031113dzbd002rf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
