\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-HKMA has no qualms keeping HK$ interest rates low.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Azhar Sukri   \par \uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2003\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be happy to keep Hong Kong dollar interbank rates lower than those for the U.S. dollar as this would help to encourage consumption, the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "With continuing deflation... we do not mind seeing Hong Kong dollar interest rates being kept low, and lower than U.S. dollar interest rates," HKMA chief Joseph Yam said in his weekly column published on the HKMA's Web site.   \par \par \uc2 Overnight Hong Kong dollar interbank rates have fallen to nearly zero since late September when the Hong Kong dollar staged a highly unusual appreciation against the U.S. dollar, driven by speculation that the Chinese yuan could be revalued following intense pressure from industrialised nations.   \par \par \uc2 This has left Hong Kong dollar interest rates about one full percentage point below those for the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "Low interest rates will hopefully encourage consumption and credit expansion, and help stabilise and perhaps inflate asset prices and the economy," Yam said.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong has suffered from five years of deflation, which has sapped companies' profitability and driven up unemployment to record levels.   \par \par \uc2 While deflation and unemployment have improved slightly over recent months, they remain significant barriers to sustainable economic growth over the next few years.   \par \par \uc2 The drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar interest rates has been a result of numerous \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the HKMA to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars and buy U.S. dollars to stabilise the local unit.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar firmed to about HK$7.70 late in September compared with its pegged exchange rate of HK$7.80.   \par \par \uc2 Under the territory's 20-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board regime, the HKMA is tasked with maintaining the stability of the Hong Kong dollar.   \par \par \uc2 While it is obliged to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening below the HK$7.80 per dollar level, it is not mandated to prevent it from strengthening above that level, as long as the exchange rate is kept relatively stable.   \par \par \uc2 The HKMA has bought a total of US$400 million against the local unit this week.   \par \par \uc2 As a result, the aggregate balance, a key measure of interbank liquidity, will expand to HK$7.4 billion on November 17 from HK$5.8 billion on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar was little moved against the greenback on Thursday following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trading at HK$7.7640/50 per U.S. dollar late on Thursday compared with the previous day's Hong Kong close, despite weakening shortly after the HKMA's latest move.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031113dzbd00260\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
