\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: HKMA Again Moves To Weaken Hong Kong Dollar Rate  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Batson  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2003\par 10:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday again sold Hong Kong dollars, and bought U.S. dollars, giving fresh impetus to the weakening of the Hong Kong dollar's exchange rate, already under way after its sales earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 In its latest transaction, the HKMA sold HK$776 million, buying about US$100 million, apparently during the lunch hour Wednesday. The move had its desired effect, moving up the exchange rate: the U.S. dollar was quoted around HK$7.7630 late Wednesday, after the injection, compared with around HK$7.7540 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Yet even before the HKMA's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, the U.S. dollar was falling against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, after the HKMA sold HK$1.55 billion in two transactions earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems the government is quite aggressive in the U.S. dollar market right now," a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said.  \par \par \uc2 "It doesn't want very much volatility in the market."  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar's spot exchange rate has been stronger than its peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar since late September, an unusual situation that has been driven by a combination of strong foreign investor interest in Hong Kong's stock and property markets, and speculation on the possible appreciation of the Chinese yuan, using the Hong Kong dollar as a proxy.  \par \par \uc2 Since the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't freely tradable, market participants often use the Hong Kong dollar as the closest available equivalent, even though the Hong Kong dollar's link to the U.S. dollar means there is no real tie to the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Though the spot rate has been gradually weakening back toward the peg level over the past month, generally trading between HK$7.75 and HK$7.77, the HKMA's latest sales were sparked by a sudden strengthening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "We think the much more active interbank market has played a significant role in the latest Hong Kong dollar rally, as two large back-to-back IPOs from China have started trading and frozen proceeds have returned to the money market," Credit Suisse First Boston economists wrote in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, PICC Property & Casualty Co. (2328.HK) debuted on the Hong Kong stock exchange after an initial public offering that raised HK$5.41 billion, and China Resources Power Holdings (0836.HK) started trading Wednesday after a HK$2.58 billion share sale.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA's injection of new funds into the banking system helped push down local interest rates, which were already near zero. The one-month Hong Kong interbank offered rate, or Hibor, was quoted at 0.05%, down from 0.10% Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Theoretically, the low interest rates should also contribute to the weakening of the Hong Kong back toward the peg rate, as they encourage investors to move out of the Hong Kong dollar and seek higher returns elsewhere.  \par \par \uc2 Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the HKMA is obliged to buy the Hong Kong dollar from banks at the peg rate, which essentially puts a floor on the Hong Kong dollar's exchange rate against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 However, the HKMA, which acts as the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has no such obligation to sell the Hong Kong dollar at the peg rate, meaning there is no automatic mechanism to prevent the exchange rate from becoming stronger than the peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 Such sales are only made at the HKMA's discretion. The HKMA doesn't provide guidelines as to when it might be willing to sell the Hong Kong dollar, saying only that it acts as needed to counter market volatility.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031112dzbc000gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
