\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Sells HK$776 Mln In New  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  In HK Dlr Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2003\par 07:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold HK$776 million Wednesday, and bought about US$100 million, in a fresh move to stem the local unit's strengthening against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said the transaction appeared to have occurred during the lunch hour Wednesday, and around the level of HK$7.7610. The U.S. dollar was quoted around HK$7.7590 before the sale, and around HK$7.7630 afterward.  \par \par \uc2 The sale came as the U.S. dollar was already rising against the Hong Kong dollar. After a sudden dive in the rate late Monday, the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell HK$1.55 billion in two transactions, buying around US$200 million.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems the government is quite aggressive in the U.S. dollar market right now," the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. "Perhaps it doesn't want very much volatility in the market."  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar's spot exchange rate has been stronger than its peg to the U.S. dollar at HK$7.80 since late September, because of massive inflows of funds to Hong Kong and market speculation that the Chinese yuan may be allowed to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the HKMA is obliged to buy the Hong Kong dollar from banks at the peg rate, which essentially puts a floor on the Hong Kong dollar's exchange rate against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 However, the HKMA, which acts as the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has no such obligation to sell the Hong Kong dollar at the peg rate, meaning there is no automatic mechanism to prevent the exchange rate from becoming stronger than the peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 Such sales are only made at the HKMA's discretion. The HKMA doesn't provide guidelines as to when it might be willing to sell the Hong Kong dollar, saying only that it acts as needed to counter market volatility.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031112dzbc0008a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
