\par\par \b \uc2 HK Dlr Mkts Late: HK Dlr Pares Gains After HKMA Moves  \b0\par\par\uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2003\par 09:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar gave up some of its overnight gains Tuesday after repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar had been trading in a tight range between HK$7.76 and HK$7.77 in recent days before a sudden dive to near HK$7.73 overnight, at which point the HKMA bought U.S. dollars to stem its fall. This was followed by further injections Tuesday morning. All in all, the HKMA sold HK$1.55 billion over Monday and Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The fall of the U.S. dollar was rumored to have been triggered by one U.S. house, which could be buying the Hong Kong dollar in relation to upcoming IPO activities," one money trader said. "But it could also have been just profit-taking of U.S. dollar positions after it failed to break HK$7.77 resistance."  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the impact of these U.S. dollar sales was amplified given that they took place in thin trading after the market closed Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The slide was quickly reversed after the HKMA's first sale of HK$775 million overnight. Additional injections totaling the same amount Tuesday morning gave a further boost to the U.S. dollar, which was quoted at HK$7.7539 late Tuesday, still below its Monday afternoon level of HK$7.7695.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the U.S. dollar to settle in a range of HK$7.73 to HK$7.77 over coming weeks as the HKMA has signaled its unwillingness to tolerate too much deviation from HK$7.80, the level at which the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong interbank offered rates fell, led by long-end Hibors, due to the extra funds injected by the HKMA at a time when the market was already flushed with liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The one-month Hibor was quoted at 0.10%, compared with 0.11% Monday. The 12-month Hibor was quoted at 1.11%, down from 1.31% previously.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong dollar forward discounts widened, reflecting the decline in Hibors and the Hong Kong dollar's gains against its U.S. counterpart in the spot market.  \par \par \uc2 The one-year contract was quoted at a discount of 330-310, compared with 240 to 220 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong Exchange Fund note yields fell moderately following forwards.  \par \par \uc2 The 10-year EF note was quoted at 4.47%, compared with 4.50% Monday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                   Latest        Late Monday \par Overnight Hibor    0.02%          0.02% \par 1-mo Hibor         0.10%          0.11% \par 3-mo Hibor         0.30%          0.40% \par 1-yr Hibor         1.11%          1.31% \par USD/HKD Spot       HK$7.7539      HK$7.7695 \par 6-mo HKD Fwd       -280 to -265   -210 to -190 \par 1-yr HKD Fwd       -330 to -310   -240 to -220 \par  \par 3.73% EF Note Due Jun. 5, 2013: \par  \par    Price: 94.63 Vs 94.37 \par    Yield: 4.47% Vs 4.50% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; raymond.tsoi@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1053 4178  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031111dzbb000cx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
