\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: HKMA Sells HK$1.5 Bln As HK Dlr Strengthens Again  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Batson and Raymond Tsoi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2003\par 07:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Facing a surge in selling of the U.S. dollar against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in an attempt to prevent the local unit from strengthening further.  \par \par \uc2 Though the HKMA doesn't comment on its market activities, it does provide information on its transactions to financial data systems. Those show it sold HK$1.5 billion, in two transactions of HK$775 million each, equivalent to buying around US$200 million.  \par \par \uc2 A trader in Hong Kong said Tuesday the first sale happened when the U.S. dollar fell to around HK$7.73. It came amid heavy selling of the U.S. dollar after the Hong Kong market closed Monday, and was later followed by the second sale. The U.S. dollar had been trading between HK$7.76 and HK$7.77 over the last few days before the sell-off.  \par \par \uc2 "The sale of the U.S. dollar for the Hong Kong dollar was rumored to have been initiated by a U.S. house, which could be related to upcoming IPO activities," a money trader said. "But it could also just have been profit-taking of U.S. dollar positions after it failed to break the resistance at HK$7.77."  \par \par \uc2 Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the HKMA is obliged to buy the Hong Kong dollar from banks at the peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar, which essentially puts a floor on the Hong Kong dollar's exchange rate against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 However, the HKMA, which acts as the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has no such obligation to sell the Hong Kong dollar at the peg rate, meaning there is no automatic mechanism to prevent the exchange rate from becoming stronger than the peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 Such sales are only made at the HKMA's discretion. The HKMA doesn't provide guidelines as to when it might be willing to sell the Hong Kong dollar, saying only that it acts as needed to counter market volatility.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar strengthened from the peg rate Sept. 22, when there was heavy selling of the U.S. dollar against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. dollar was quoted as low as HK$7.71 in the immediate aftermath, but since then its exchange rate with the Hong Kong dollar has moved gradually back up toward the peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA has sold Hong Kong dollars occasionally since then, though analysts say it hasn't been trying to actively guide the spot rate back to the peg rate. Rather, it has acted only intermittently to keep the rate from going below a certain level.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA's move did have some effect on the market: the U.S. dollar was quoted around HK$7.7525 early Tuesday afternoon, compared with HK$7.7480 earlier in the day, though the rate is still down compared with HK$7.7695 Monday afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson and Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031111dzbb00080\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
