\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  as HK dlr firms, discount widens.   \b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2003\par 05:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 HONG KONG\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Nov 11 (Reuters) -The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  twice on Tuesday morning to try to stabilise the\par territory's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but it remained stubbornly strong against\par the U.S. dollar, to which it is pegged.\par     In the latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the HKMA sold HK$775 million\par (US$100 million) in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday. It also sold\par HK$775 million (US$100 million) on Monday during London trading\par hours.\par     The local unit was trading at 7.7525 at 0332 GMT versus a\par high of 7.7380 in London trade on Monday after Hong Kong's market\par close.\par     The Hong Kong dollar strengthened suddenly in late September.\par Some dealers attributed the change to growing signs of an\par economic recovery and mounting international political pressure\par on Beijing to introduce greater flexibility in its exchange rate.\par  \par     "I think there is some hot money coming in for initial public\par offerings of stock," said a dealer at a European bank.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "I think the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will remain strong with interbank\par rates continuing in a downward trend," a dealer from a Japanese\par bank said.\par     The HKMA, the territory's de facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is obligated\par to defend the Hong Kong dollar from weakening below HK$7.80, but\par is not obliged to prevent it from strengthening above that level.\par     Hong Kong dollar forwards have been slipping to a deeper\par discount since last Friday.\par     At 0340 GMT, the discount on one-year forwards widened to\par 370/340 pips from 255/240 pips at 0951 GMT on Monday, versus\par Thursday's close of 135/120 pips.\par     There was speculation that the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might move in\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 tandem with the yuan should the latter be revalued. Hong Kong, a   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 former British colony, has been forging ever closer economic ties   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 with mainland China since it reverted to Chinese rule in 1997.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing has so far resisted revaluation or a widening of the   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 trading band, saying it must deal with growing unemployment and   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 creaky state banks and government-owned firms first. But it says   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 it plans to float the yuan eventually.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the cash market interbank rates fell across the board on   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the aggregate balance, a key measure of interbank liquidity,   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 which is set to increase to HK$5.851 billion on Thursday from   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HK$4.261 billion on Tuesday.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The one-year rate was quoted at 1.10-1.13 percent, down from\par 1.20-1.25 percent on Monday.\par                      Morning       Previous Close\par                      At 0350 GMT   At 0951 GMT\par     HK$ SPOT         7.7520/23     7.7670/75\par     FORWARDS\par     Three-month      -183 / -173   -165 / -155\par     Six-month        -300 / -285   -245 / -235\par     One-year         -370 / -340   -255 / -240\par     INTERBANK RATE (PERCENTAGE)\par     Overnight       0.005 / 0.01   0.01 / 0.01\par     Three-month      0.20 / 