\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: HKMA Relaxed About Stronger HK Dollar, Firm On Peg  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Batson  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2003\par 09:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sees no threat to the territory's 20-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, which links the Hong Kong dollar to the U.S. dollar, from the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening in recent weeks above the fixed rate of 7.80.  \par \par \uc2 "Our somewhat relaxed attitude toward small deviations in the exchange rate on the strong side of 7.80 does not mean that we are any less committed to maintaining the link," said HKMA chief executive Joseph Yam in a speech Tuesday evening.  \par \par \uc2 "There is an effective mechanism for us to ensure that these fluctuations are not so large as to undermine credibility and confidence in the link," he said.  \par \par \uc2 In separate remarks to reporters earlier Tuesday, Yam said that the HKMA's priority in this unusual situation isn't targeting a particular exchange rate, but rather maintaining the overall confidence of investors and the public in the peg system.  \par \par \uc2 While Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system has an automatic mechanism to prevent depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it doesn't have any such arrangement to prevent appreciation. In order to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the HKMA must make transactions, at its discretion, in which it sells the Hong Kong dollar and buys the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, though Yam didn't allude to it, the HKMA did so Tuesday, selling HK$969 million into the market when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was near the 7.75 level. But that move and Yam's comments had little effect on the spot exchange rate, and the Hong Kong dollar strengthened to around 7.7440 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars and buy U.S. dollars on four days since the USD/HKD exchange rate dropped below 7.80 on Sept. 22, injecting a total of HK$3.75 billion into the market.  \par \par \uc2 "Minor fluctuations in the market exchange rate on the strong side of the link are a feature of our monetary system, and they are nothing to get too excited about," Yam said in his speech, a written copy of which was provided by the government.  \par \par \uc2 Not everyone agrees. Michael Spencer, an economist at Deutsche Bank, argues that by permitting a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the HKMA has changed the rules of the game. "The understanding was that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was fixed at 7.8. We're clearly no longer in the same regime we were three weeks ago," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Of course, such complaints can be dismissed as sour grapes from those who had been speculating on a future depreciation of the Hong Kong dollar, and got caught out when the spot exchange rate unexpectedly strengthened in response to speculation about appreciation of the Chinese yuan and by selling of the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Since that happened on Sept. 22, analysts say the HKMA has indeed been "relaxed" about returning the exchange rate to 7.80, as its sales haven't been big or frequent enough to give the rate much of a push back toward 7.80. They note that a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps keep down interest rates, and also contributes to the recent increased optimism about Hong Kong's economic prospects.  \par \par \uc2 Other critics have pointed out that Hong Kong has endured years of deflation in order to bring its prices down to more competitive levels, an achievement that could be threatened if the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues. Yam, however, downplayed the significance of a slightly stronger Hong Kong dollar, saying it was outweighed by the broader weakness of the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "An orderly depreciation of the U.S. (dollar) against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in Hong Kong's interests," Yam said in the speech. "Because Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is linked to the U.S. dollar, Hong Kong also becomes more competitive."  \par \par \uc2 "This larger point may have been overlooked in all of the fuss about the somewhat less significant strengthening of the Hong Kong dollar against the U.S. dollar," he said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031015dzaf000ga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
