\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 14  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 11:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Bank of Japan Tuesday upgraded its economic assessment for a second consecutive month but said the yen's appreciation and a weak U.S. labor market threaten a pickup in exports. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since January didn't describe Japan's economy as "flat", cautiously acknowledging that a recovery is in sight. Still, there is uncertainty about exports "as the employment situation in the U.S. continues to be sluggish and the yen exchange rate has been unstable, tending toward appreciation against the U.S. dollar," it added. ("=WRAP: BOJ Upgrades Econ Outlook But Wary Of Yen Impact" ran at 0743 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: There is no reason for the yen to strengthen due to an upcoming visit to Tokyo by U.S. President George W. Bush, as U.S. officials have repeated their commitment to a strong dollar, says a senior Japanese Finance Ministry official. Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi told reporters it is unlikely Bush's visit would be further reason to sway \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or make the yen gain against the dollar, as some in the market are currently speculating will happen. Bush will be in Tokyo Friday and Saturday. ("Japan Mizoguchi" first take ran at 0043 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China is to cut the tax rebates it pays to exporters by an average three percentage points as part of an attempt to reduce the drain on the country's finances. But it is a move that might also help disarm criticism of its fixed exchange rate policy. ("("=WRAP: China Cuts Export Rebate To Deflect Yuan Criticism" ran at 0348 GMT)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quasi \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if it is necessary to maintain the confidence of the markets and the public in the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief executive Joseph Yam said Tuesday. ("HKMA's Yam/ \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  -2: No Specific Target For \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " first take ran at 0839 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China is facing a barrage of political arm-twisting from the U.S. to raise the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan. But a growing source of upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes from its own backyard: Hong Kong. ("=Cheap HK Loans, Yuan Expectations Fuel China Speculation" ran at 0708 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: If Thailand acts on a threat to tax speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades, it's unlikely to be effective in curbing upward pressure on the baht, analysts say. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tax Likely Wouldn't Curb Baht Rise" ran at 0323 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Cheung Kong (Holdings) said Tuesday that it will begin its planned HK$10 billion retail bond program with HK$800 million in bonds denominated in Hong Kong and Australian dollars. ("Cheung Kong/Bonds" first take ran at 0739 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian independent power producer SKS Power Sdn. Bhd. will issue around 1.5 billion ringgit worth of Islamic bonds in November, banking sources told Dow Jones Tuesday. The issue is the first tranche of a MYR5.6 billion bond program, which will be the largest deal in the country this year. ("=Malaysia SKS/Bonds" ran at 0345 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The timeline for a rise in Australian interest rates continues to shorten with economists nominating a hike in December as economic conditions revive from a second- quarter slump. Recent data suggests capacity constraints may soon become a factor for policy-makers in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  while demand in the housing sector remains unrelenting. ("=WRAP: Timeline For Australian Rate Hike Shortening" ran at 0557 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's domestic corporate goods prices fell 0.5% on year in September, the Bank of Japan said Tuesday, marking the 37th straight month of decline and showing the economy remains under persistent deflationary pressure. ("Japan Sep Corp Price Index" first take ran at 2358 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's fourth-largest life insurer, Meiji Life Insurance, will buy more foreign bonds in the next six months without hedging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk after building a risk-taking buffer in the first half to Sept. 30. ("=INTERVIEW: Meiji Life To Buy More Foreign Bonds Unhedged" ran at 0513 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Consumer sentiment in South Korea fell to its lowest level in six months in September, with most still pessimistic about economic conditions through to at least the first quarter of next year. South Korea's consumer sentiment index was tallied at 90.4 in September, down from 92 in August. ("S Korea's Sept Consumer Sentiment Lowest In Six Months" first take ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: The dollar's discount against the Indian rupee in the forwards market is likely to steepen over the next few weeks given a bearish outlook toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bankers said Tuesday. The dollar's discount has sunk to its deepest level ever in forward instruments up to 12 months, with the benchmark one-year dollar swinging to discount for the first time early Tuesday. ("=Forward Dollar Discount Vs India Rupee Likely To Steepen" ran at 0708 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =INSIDE INDOCHINA: Five years after U.S. negotiators first sought a bilateral aviation agreement with Vietnam, news came last week of a deal that will allow direct flights between the two countries for the first time since the end of the Vietnam War in 1975. Vietnam Airlines will be the first to benefit from the deal, which has been heralded as a major step toward faster and cheaper trade and tourism ties. ("=INSIDE INDOCHINA: Air Pact A Boost For Vietnam Airlines" ran at 0533 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MACAU: Trade officials from Portugal and several of its former colonies wrapped up a three-day forum Tuesday, pledging to seek increased trade and investment with China by using Macau, the former Portuguese enclave now ruled by China, as a gateway. (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Approved foreign direct investment in Indonesia rose 3.7% on year to $6.16 billion in the first nine months of 2003, the Indonesian investment board said Tuesday. ("Approved FDI In Indonesia Up 3.7% To $6.16 Bln In Jan-Sep" ran at 0156 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Economic conditions in New Zealand are very similar to what they were in 1996, just before the economy suffered a major growth slowdown and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged, said Paul Dwyer, chief investment officer of AMP Henderson Global Investors. ("NZ Economy Like 1996, But Correction Won't Be So Bad -AMP" on wires)  \par \par \uc2 COLOMBO: Sri Lanka's private sector needs to take bolder steps to develop the corporate bond market, capitalizing on efforts made by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on a recovery in the economy, a senior executive in the finance sector said. ("=INTERVIEW:Sri Lanka Debt Mkt Needs Backing Of Pvt Sector" ran at 0833 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar posted strong gains against the euro and rose more modestly against the yen in Asia Tuesday, boosted by buybacks triggered by hopes for further upbeat U.S. economic data. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0553 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Deutsche Telekom has made a new offer to Singapore Telecommunications and Philippine conglomerate Ayala Corp. to sell its 24.8% stake in Globe Telecom. SingTel didn't disclose financial details, but said it and Ayala would evaluate the offer and respond within 45 days. Globe shareholders SingTel and Ayala in August let an option to buy Deutsche Telekom's stake in Globe expire, after failing to agree on a price. Globe was one of SingTel's first acquisitions in its S$18 billion spending spree on mobile and fixed line telecommunications investments in Asia outside Singapore. ("SingTel/Ayala/Deutsche" ran at 0041 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Seiyu Tuesday reported a group net loss of Y8.43 billion for the fiscal first half ended Aug. 30, as a cold wet summer damped sales in all its product categories. But that was still better than last year's first-half loss of Y22.39 billion. Last year, the Japanese supermarket chain operator affiliated with U.S. retail giant Wal-Mart Stores had to post large restructuring charges that resulted in hefty losses, while the impact on earnings of introducing Wal-Mart's marketing methods was still limited. ("=WRAP: Seiyu Posts 1H Loss After Bad Weather Damps Sales" ran at 0830 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Conglomerate CITIC Pacific has bought 360.96 million yuan of power generation and power equipment manufacturing assets from its parent, saying "the acquisition represents an excellent opportunity for the group to further expand its power business in mainland China, picking assets attractive to the group." (WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN IPO FOCUS: China non-life insurer PICC Property & Casualty Co. expects to go public in Hong Kong next month in one of Asia's biggest initial public offerings this year, hoping that investors will regard it as an attractive route into China's huge insurance market. ("=ASIAN IPO FOCUS: PICC Takes IPO Route To China Insur Mkt" ran at 0321 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ADELAIDE: Southcorp Tuesday sought patience from shareholders as the ailing Australian winemaker embarks on a restructuring amid speculation it might soon face a A$2 billion-plus takeover bid. ("=WRAP: Australia's Southcorp Seeks Shareholder Support" ran at 0828 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australian automotive parts retailer Repco Corp. is expected to lodge details of a A$500 million initial public share offer as early as this week, joining the race for new investment dollars. In one of the largest public offerings in Australia this year, Repco shares may start trading on the Australian Stock Exchange later next month, people with knowledge of the sale said Tuesday. ("=WRAP: Australia's Repco Revving Up For A$500 Mln IPO" ran at 0700 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Sony Corp. won't advance the release of its Playstation video game console in China to head off competition from rival Nintendo, a Sony executive said Tuesday. (INTERVIEW will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Turning the tables on market expectations, it now looks as though government-owned Internet service provider Jaring may acquire its larger TMNet rival, a division of telecommunication giant Telekom Malaysia, people familiar with the deal told Dow Jones Tuesday. ("=Jaring Could Lead In Merger With Malaysia TMNet-Sources" ran at 0557 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: ProMOS Technologies, Taiwan's third-largest maker of computer memory chips, said Tuesday it posted a net profit of NT$864 million in the third quarter on solid chip prices, its first quarterly profit for this year. ("Taiwan's ProMOS/Earnings" first take ran at 0659 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Shanghai No. 1 Department Store, the city's largest, has hired an international property consulting firm to restructure its property, and perhaps open its doors to foreign retailers. Cushman & Wakefield says it has a contract to reposition the store as a world-class retail property. (story will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Shanghai Automotive Industry Corp., the joint venture partner of both GM and Volkswagen in China, said sales rose 42% in the first nine months of the year. (story will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The receivers of troubled property developer Shanghai Land said Tuesday they are considering a capital reorganization plan that includes a special cash distribution to shareholders before selling a controlling stake in the company. ("Shanghai Land Receivers Mull Stake Sale, Special Dividend" ran at 0054 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The NZ$15 million initial public offering of shares in 42 Below Ltd., a New Zealand vodka and gin distiller, was fully subscribed and some investors who didn't receive allocations from stock exchange firms will miss out, the company said Tuesday. ("NZ's 42 Below Offer Fully Subscribed, To List Wednesday" ran at 2250 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The rising Australian dollar and worries over the global economy continue to weigh on the borrowing intentions of the country's top 500 corporations, JP Morgan and East & Partners said Tuesday in their latest quarterly banking survey. ("Rising A$ Dents Top Co's Borrowing Intentions - Survey" ran at 0305 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Siam Cement, Thailand's largest industrial conglomerate, is looking for investment opportunities to expand in Southeast Asia over the next three years. ("=INTERVIEW: Siam Cement Eyes Invest In Southeast Asia" ran at 0634 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TECHNICAL TALK: Shares of Singapore Press Holdings are higher Tuesday, and while further immediate gains may not be likely, technical charts suggest more upside over the longer term.("=TECHNICAL TALK:Singapore Press Hldgs Resistance At S$21" ran at 0547 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Southern Airlines said Tuesday it carried 2.18 million passengers in September, up 20.8% from a year earlier. ("China Southern's Sep Passenger Numbers Up 20.8% On Yr" ran at 0240 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hainan Meilan Airport said passenger traffic at the Haikou International Airport was 23% higher in the Oct 1-7 period than a year earlier due to an increase in travel during the National Day holiday week. ("China Meilan Airport Passenger Traffic Up 23% In Oct 1-7" ran at 0139 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand government-owned power generator and retailer Mighty River Tuesday reported income after tax for the financial year ended June of NZ$113.5 million, compared with a NZ$47.1 million surplus a year earlier. ("NZ Mighty River Power FY Net NZ$113.5M Vs NZ$47.1M" ran at 2358 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Wool Equities, a rural investment company, will become the first company to list on the New Zealand Exchange's new market for small companies when it opens next month. ("NZ's Wool Equities To Be First To List On New NZAX Market" ran at 2217 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese power utility Hokkaido Electric Power will raise the operating rates at its oil-fired power plants to full capacity to cover an expected supply shortage in winter due to the shutdown of its two nuclear reactors. ("Japan's Hokkaido Elec To Up Output From Oil-Fired Plants" ran at 0525 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's Medco Energi International is continuing its search for available oil and natural gas blocks at home and in Malaysia, Vietnam and Myanmar to boost output and counter falling reserves, a company official said Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW:Indonesia Medco Eyeing Oil,Gas Blocks Overseas" ran at 0830 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031014dzae000d9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
